repl_fork — evaluation
=======================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_fork (plans/repl_fork)

task
----
explore two proof strategies without losing the common prefix —
the branching workflow fork exists for.

setup
-----
fresh server. the lemma is chosen so both routes are plausible and
at least one needs a few steps (e.g. a list lemma where induction
works and a lemma-lookup/simp route also exists).

prompt
------
"Prove `lemma \"length (concat xss) = sum_list (map length xss)\"`
in Isabelle/HOL. I want you to try TWO genuinely different proof
strategies (e.g. induction vs. rewriting with library lemmas),
compare where each gets stuck or succeeds, and give me the Isar
text of the better one. Set up the lemma statement once — don't
redo the common groundwork for each attempt."

success
-------
both strategies were actually explored from a shared prefix; the
final answer's Isar text is verified in one of the branches.

signals
-------
- does the agent fork, or run strategy A, truncate/back to the
  start, run strategy B (destructive sequential exploration — works,
  loses the comparison), or create two repls from scratch repeating
  the lemma statement (ignores the "don't redo groundwork"
  instruction)? each workaround grades selection differently.
- state_idx use: default tip vs explicit index; if it passes a
  negative index, does the n+1+idx semantics (plans/repl_fork T2)
  match its expectation?
- cleanup behavior after choosing a winner (feeds evals/repl_remove
  and evals/repl_merge — note it, don't grade it here).

reflection
----------
- if agents never fork unprompted, the description must sell the
  use case ("try alternatives without losing your place") harder,
  or branching belongs in a skill.
- if two-repls-from-scratch is the dominant natural strategy, fork's
  value claim is thin for agents (unlike humans) — existence-ladder
  question.
